  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening rural community support systems to sustain care and support</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Kenya has been badly affected by the HIV epidemic. In the county of Bungoma in Western Kenya, HIV rates are very high. This is made worse by very high levels of poverty, compounded by the recent high inflation rates.  Bungoma is on the Trans-Africa Highway, with stop-over towns used by long distance truck drivers, one of the population groups most at risk of contracting HIV.  ACE Africa has worked in Bungoma since 2004. This project will phase out work in eight communities and expand to four new areas.  The project will continue to protect vulnerable children, provide care and support for people living with HIV, including educational and vocational training and nutritional and agricultural support for the whole community.  The project will expand its work with people who are most at risk of contracting HIV such as people with disabilities, sugar cane workers, long distance lorry drivers, prisoners and sex workers. The main focus in this last phase will be to improve the quality of local services, working closely with the local government health and welfare systems, creating an integrated and lasting system of care and support for people living with, and affected by HIV.</narrative>
  </description>
